          content: `# Components and Props

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Components are the heart of React. A component is a reusable, self-contained piece of UI that accepts inputs called props (short for properties) and returns a React element describing what should appear on screen. Understanding components and props is fundamental because every React application is essentially a tree of components passing data down through props.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Props are like function arguments for your components. When you call a function, you pass arguments to customize its behavior. Similarly, when you use a component, you pass props to customize what it renders. The key rule is that props are read-only â€” a component must never modify its own props. This constraint enforces the principle of unidirectional data flow and makes applications predictable and easier to debug. If data needs to change, it must flow from a parent component that manages state.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
The props pattern enforces a clean separation of concerns. Parent components manage state and logic, while child components focus on presentation. This separation makes components reusable across different contexts, simplifies testing (each component can be tested with mock props), and improves code maintainability by keeping data flow explicit and traceable.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Props can be any JavaScript value: strings, numbers, booleans, objects, arrays, functions, or even React elements. Use destructuring in the function parameter to access specific props. Default props can be set via default parameters. Children â€” content passed between opening and closing tags â€” is accessed via the special children prop. Props drilling (passing props through many layers) can become unwieldy, which is where context and state management solutions come in. Always validate props with TypeScript interfaces or PropTypes for better developer experience and bug prevention.`,
