          type: "theory",
          content: `# Component Composition Patterns

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Component composition is the practice of building complex UIs by combining smaller, focused components. React's composition model is flexible and powerful â€” it encourages you to split your UI into small, single-purpose components that can be combined in various ways. Mastering composition patterns is what separates novice React developers from experienced ones.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Think of composition like nesting Russian dolls or combining LEGO bricks. A small, simple brick (component) can be combined with other bricks to build something complex. The special children prop allows components to be truly generic containers â€” like a Card component that wraps whatever content you put inside it. This pattern eliminates the need for complex inheritance hierarchies that are common in object-oriented UI frameworks.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Good composition leads to highly reusable code. When you design components to be composable, you create a library of building blocks that can be assembled in countless ways across your application. This reduces duplication, improves consistency, and makes your codebase easier to maintain. Well-composed components are also easier to test in isolation.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
There are several key composition patterns. **Container/Presenter** splits data fetching (container) from rendering (presenter). **Higher-Order Components** wrap a component to add behavior. **Render Props** pass a rendering function via props. **Compound Components** (like <Select><Option>) share implicit state via context. The children prop is the most basic composition tool â€” it lets components act as generic wrappers. Slot-based composition (passing multiple named sections via props) is useful for complex layouts like modals or dashboards.`,
