          type: "theory",
          content: `# useState and State Management

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
The useState hook is the most fundamental React hook. It adds state to functional components, allowing them to remember data between renders and trigger re-renders when data changes. Understanding useState deeply is essential because it forms the foundation for all other hooks and state management patterns in React.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Imagine a light switch. It has two states: on and off. When you flip it, the light changes. useState works the same way â€” it gives your component memory. The component remembers the current state, and when you call the update function (like flipping the switch), React automatically re-renders the component to reflect the new state. Each call to useState creates a separate piece of memory for your component.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
State is what makes components dynamic and interactive. Without state, components are static templates that never change. With state, components can respond to user input, display loading indicators, toggle content visibility, manage form data, and much more. Mastering useState â€” including its nuances with stale closures and batching â€” prevents many common React bugs.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
The useState hook returns an array with two elements: the current state value and a setter function. State updates are asynchronous and batched in React 18+. The setter can accept a new value or a callback function that receives the previous state. When updating objects or arrays, always create new references rather than mutating existing ones. React uses Object.is comparison to determine if state changed before re-rendering. For expensive initial state computations, pass a function to useState instead of a value to avoid re-executing on every render.`,
