          type: "theory",
          content: `# useEffect and Side Effects

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
The useEffect hook handles side effects in React components: fetching data, subscribing to events, manipulating the DOM, and setting timers. It replaces the lifecycle methods of class components (componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate, componentWillUnmount) with a unified, dependency-driven API.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Think of useEffect as a way to step outside React's rendering world to do things that have nothing to do with rendering. Rendering is like preparing a meal on a plate â€” it is declarative and predictable. Side effects are like calling a friend to ask for a recipe, setting a timer to check the oven, or cleaning up crumbs after eating. These actions happen outside the core rendering flow but are essential for real applications.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Side effects are where most bugs happen in React applications. Missing dependency arrays cause infinite loops. Missing cleanup functions cause memory leaks. Race conditions in async effects cause stale state updates. Mastering useEffect is critical for building reliable, production-quality applications that interact with the outside world.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
The useEffect hook takes two arguments: a callback function and an optional dependency array. When dependencies change, the callback re-runs. With an empty array [], it runs once after the initial render (mount). With no array, it runs after every render. The callback may return a cleanup function that runs before the next effect run and when the component unmounts. Modern React (18+) uses strict mode to double-invoke effects in development, helping detect missing cleanup functions. For async operations, use an internal flag to prevent stale state updates after unmount.`,
