          title: "useContext and the Context API",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# useContext and the Context API

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
React Context provides a way to share data across the component tree without passing props through every level. The useContext hook consumes context values in functional components. Context is ideal for global data like themes, authentication status, locale preferences, and UI state that many components need access to.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Props are like handing a note to the person next to you and asking them to pass it along. Context is like posting the note on a bulletin board where anyone can read it directly. Instead of threading props through five layers of components (prop drilling), context lets any component in the tree access the data directly. This is much cleaner for data that many components need, but it should not be used for everything â€” just like you would not post every private conversation on a bulletin board.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Prop drilling makes code hard to maintain and refactor. When a deeply nested component needs data from a top-level ancestor, you end up passing props through components that do not use them, just to deliver them deeper. Context eliminates this problem. However, overusing context can make component reuse harder and cause unnecessary re-renders, so understanding when to use it is crucial.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Creating context involves three steps. First, create the context with React.createContext (optionally with a default value). Second, provide the value with a Provider component that wraps the subtree. Third, consume the value with useContext anywhere inside the Provider. Context triggers re-renders in all consumers when the value changes. To optimize, split context into separate concerns (ThemeContext, AuthContext) and memoize provider values. Context is not a state management solution â€” it is a dependency injection mechanism. Combine it with useReducer for local state management patterns.`,
