          title: "Lifting State Up",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# Lifting State Up

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Lifting state up is a foundational pattern where shared state is moved to the closest common ancestor of components that need it. Instead of each child managing its own copy of related data, the parent holds the source of truth and passes data and callbacks down via props.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Imagine two siblings each have a TV remote. They can change their own channel, but they cannot see what the other is watching. If you want them to watch the same channel, you give one remote to the parent, and the parent tells both siblings what channel to watch. Lifting state up works the same way â€” the parent holds the shared state and passes it down to children through props.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Without lifting state, components end up with conflicting or duplicated state. Two inputs that should be synchronized, a master-detail view, or a form with multiple interdependent fields all require shared state. Lifting state up is the simplest and most React-idiomatic solution for these scenarios. It keeps data flow explicit and components predictable.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Identify shared state by looking for components that need to reflect the same data. Move the useState call to their lowest common ancestor. Pass the state value as a prop to children that display it. Pass setter callbacks to children that modify it. This pattern works well for small to medium component trees. When the tree grows too deep, consider Context or external state management. Lifting state up also simplifies testing because the stateful logic is concentrated in fewer components.`,
