          type: "theory",
          content: `# Introduction to Client-Side Routing

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Client-side routing lets you build single-page applications (SPAs) where navigating between views does not require a full page reload. React Router is the most popular routing library for React, providing declarative routing with URL-based navigation, browser history integration, and lazy loading support.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
In a traditional multi-page website, clicking a link sends a request to the server, which returns a new HTML page. In a SPA with client-side routing, the application intercepts the link click, updates the URL in the browser, and renders a different component â€” all without a server request. The browser never refreshes, making navigation feel instant. React Router manages this process, keeping the UI in sync with the URL.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Client-side routing is fundamental to modern web applications. It provides a better user experience (no page flashes on navigation), enables complex UIs with persistent state across views, and allows you to lazy-load routes for better performance. Understanding React Router is essential because virtually every production React application uses some form of routing.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
React Router v6 uses a declarative route configuration with nested routes, layout routes, and data loading. BrowserRouter syncs the UI with the browser's URL. Routes define the mapping between URL paths and components. Link and NavLink provide client-side navigation. useParams reads dynamic segments from the URL. useNavigate provides programmatic navigation. React Router supports nested routes that render inside parent layouts, outlet components that mark where child content renders, and route-level data loading with loaders and actions.`,
