          content: `# Route Parameters and Navigation

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Dynamic routes use URL parameters to render different content based on the URL. React Router provides useParams to read URL parameters, useNavigate for programmatic navigation, and search parameter support with useSearchParams. These tools enable patterns like product detail pages, user profiles, and filterable lists.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Think of route parameters as variables in the URL path. A route like /users/:id matches /users/42 and //users/alice â€” the :id part captures the dynamic segment. Your component reads this value with useParams and fetches or displays the appropriate data. Search parameters (URL query strings) work similarly with useSearchParams, useful for filters, pagination, and sorting state.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Dynamic routing is what makes SPAs feel like real applications. Without it, every user and every product would need its own hardcoded route. Route parameters enable a single route definition to serve unlimited dynamic content. Programmatic navigation (useNavigate) is essential for redirecting after form submissions, login flows, and guided tutorials.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Define dynamic segments with a colon prefix: /products/:productId. useParams returns an object with all matched parameters. Optional parameters use a trailing question mark: /users/:id?. Multiple parameters are supported: /categories/:catId/products/:prodId. useSearchParams returns the query string as a URLSearchParams object with a setter. useNavigate returns a navigate function that accepts a path and optional state. Navigate programmatically with navigate('/dashboard'), go back with navigate(-1), or replace history entry with navigate('/login', { replace: true }).`,
