          content: `# Nested Routes and Layouts

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Nested routes are one of React Router v6's most powerful features. They allow parent routes to wrap child routes with shared layouts, enabling consistent UI patterns like sidebars with detail views, tabs with sub-pages, and settings sections with multiple categories.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Think of nested routes like folders on your computer. The parent route is a folder (Settings), and child routes are files inside it (Profile, Account, Notifications). When you enter the Settings folder, you see a layout (sidebar + content area). Clicking a child file changes only the content area â€” the sidebar stays. Nested routes do exactly this: the parent component renders a layout with an Outlet, and child components fill that outlet.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Nested routes eliminate layout duplication. Without them, every settings sub-page would need to render the sidebar and header boilerplate. With nested routes, the layout is defined once in the parent and automatically applied to all children. This makes your code DRYer, your navigation structure clearer, and your layouts consistent across the application.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Define a parent route with child routes inside it. The parent component renders an <Outlet /> where child content appears. Parent routes can also have their own loader functions and error boundaries. Index routes render inside the outlet when the parent path is matched exactly (like a default child). Relative links in nested routes are relative to the parent route, not the root. Use relative path routing with './child-path' for cleaner route definitions. Layout routes can be nested arbitrarily deep â€” great for dashboards with multiple levels of navigation.`,
