          type: "theory",
          content: `# What is TypeScript?

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
TypeScript is a typed superset of JavaScript that compiles to plain JavaScript. It adds static type checking, interfaces, generics, and modern ECMAScript features. TypeScript catches errors at compile time rather than runtime, making large codebases more maintainable and reducing production bugs.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Imagine writing JavaScript with a spell-checker that catches mistakes as you type. TypeScript is JavaScript with a safety net. You write TypeScript code (JavaScript with types), and the compiler checks for errors before producing clean JavaScript that runs anywhere. The types disappear at runtime, leaving efficient JavaScript.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
TypeScript has become the standard for professional JavaScript development. React, Angular, and Node.js all recommend TypeScript. Studies show TypeScript catches 15% of bugs at compile time. Learning TypeScript increases your marketability and reduces debugging time.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
TypeScript's type system is structural â€” compatibility is determined by shape, not explicit declarations. The compiler offers strictness settings: noImplicitAny, strictNullChecks, strictFunctionTypes. The TypeScript compiler (tsc) can target different ECMAScript versions. TypeScript provides editor tooling: IntelliSense, refactoring, go-to-definition, and find-all-references.`,
