          title: "Basic Types and Type Annotations",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# Basic Types and Type Annotations

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
TypeScript provides type annotations for primitive types (string, number, boolean), arrays, tuples, enums, and special types (any, unknown, never, void). Understanding these basic types is essential before moving to advanced type system features.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Type annotations are labels on variables that tell TypeScript what kind of value they hold. const name: string = 'Alice' tells TypeScript that name is text. If you assign a number later, TypeScript warns you. Additional types like unknown (type-safe any) and never (impossible values) provide fine-grained control over type safety.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Mastering basic types gives you the vocabulary to describe your data shapes to TypeScript. Understanding when to use unknown vs any impacts code safety directly. Proper use of tuples and enums makes code more self-documenting and prevents errors.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
TypeScript infers types when you do not provide explicit annotations. For function parameters, explicit annotations are required unless you enable noImplicitAny. Arrays: number[] or Array<number>. Tuples: fixed-length arrays with per-position types [string, number]. Enums generate runtime code â€” prefer union types of string literals. any disables type checking entirely. unknown requires type narrowing before use. never represents values that should never occur.`,
