          content: `# Interfaces and Type Aliases

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Interfaces and type aliases define object shapes. Interfaces are extendable and support declaration merging. Type aliases support unions, intersections, and primitive type aliases. Understanding when to use each is a key TypeScript skill.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
An interface is a blueprint for an object. It defines what properties and methods an object must have. A type alias is more flexible â€” it can describe unions, intersections, and primitives. Use interfaces for objects, type aliases for everything else.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Interfaces are the backbone of TypeScript's type system for objects. They enable autocompletion, refactoring, and documentation. When you define interfaces for API responses, component props, and state shapes, you create a type-safe contract that prevents bugs.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Interfaces (interface) are primarily for object shapes and can be extended with extends. They support declaration merging. Type aliases (type) work with unions (A | B), intersections (A & B), primitives (type Name = string), and tuples. Interfaces produce better error messages. Type aliases are more versatile for complex type compositions. Optional properties use ?. Readonly properties use readonly keyword.`,
