          title: "Generic Functions",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# Generic Functions

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Generics allow you to write functions that work with any data type while maintaining type safety. Instead of writing separate functions for numbers and strings, you write one generic function that works with both and preserves the specific type.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Generics are type variables that get filled in when the function is called. function identity<T>(value: T): T preserves the input type. Call identity(42) and T becomes number. Call identity('hello') and T becomes string. The return type always matches the input type.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Generics eliminate code duplication. Without them, you need separate functions for each type or use any (losing type safety). Generics are everywhere in TypeScript: Array<T>, Promise<T>, Map<K, V>. Understanding generics is essential for effective TypeScript development.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Type parameters are in angle brackets: <T>. Multiple parameters: <K, V>. Constraints: <T extends SomeType>. Type inference fills parameters from usage, or specify explicitly: identity<string>('hello'). Default types: <T = string>. Constraints ensure type safety while allowing flexibility. Generic type parameters can be constrained to primitives, interfaces, or union types.`,
