          type: "theory",
          content: `# Type Inference and Contextual Typing

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
TypeScript infers types from values, contexts, and control flow. Contextual typing infers types from the surrounding context. Understanding inference helps you write cleaner code with fewer annotations.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Type inference is TypeScript figuring out types automatically. const x = 5 infers number. arr.map(x => x.length) infers x from the array type. Contextual typing works backward â€” the expected type in a callback parameter is inferred from the array's element type.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Good inference reduces boilerplate while maintaining safety. Contextual typing makes APIs like forEach and map feel natural. Understanding inference helps you know when annotations are necessary.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Inference stages: literals -> function bodies -> generic call sites. Contextual typing: types inferred from expected position (callback params, return types). Type narrowing: typeof, instanceof, discriminated unions narrow types in branches. The satisfies operator validates without widening. Union type narrowing via switch/case with discriminated properties.`,
