          content: `# Discriminated Unions and Exhaustive Checking

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Discriminated unions have a common discriminant property that TypeScript uses to narrow types. Exhaustive checking ensures all union variants are handled. This is the foundation of type-safe state management.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Each variant has a tag (like kind: 'circle' | 'square'). When you switch on the tag, TypeScript knows which shape each branch has. Adding a new variant without a handler causes a compile error with exhaustive checking.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Discriminated unions model states, API responses, and component variants. Exhaustive checking catches unhandled variants at compile time, preventing the most common state management bug.

ðŸ“ Detailed Explanation
Use a discriminant property with literal types. Each variant has its own properties. Exhaustive check: default case assigns state to never. If a new variant is unhandled, the assignment fails at compile time. Use pattern matching libraries like ts-pattern for complex cases.`,
