          title: "API-First Design Approach",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# API-First Design Approach

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
API-first design means designing the API contract before writing implementation code. This approach uses specification documents (OpenAPI, RAML) to define endpoints, request/response schemas, and validation rules upfront. It enables parallel development, automated testing, and better documentation.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
API-first design is like agreeing on a blueprint before building a house. All teams (frontend, backend, mobile) know exactly what the API will look like. They can work in parallel â€” frontend can use mock data while backend is being built, and everyone knows when something changes because the blueprint (spec) changes first.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
API-first prevents the most expensive type of bug: a mismatch between what the frontend expects and what the backend delivers. It also generates automatic documentation, client SDKs, and contract tests. Major companies like Stripe, Twilio, and GitHub practice API-first design.

## Benefits

- **Parallel Development**: Frontend and backend teams work simultaneously
- **Automated Documentation**: Spec becomes living documentation
- **Contract Testing**: Validate API compliance automatically
- **Client SDK Generation**: Auto-generate type-safe clients
- **Early Feedback**: Stakeholders review the API before coding begins`,
