          title: "REST API Design Patterns",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# REST API Design Patterns

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
Common REST API design patterns solve recurring problems: filtering, sorting, searching, pagination, partial responses, bulk operations, and asynchronous operations. These patterns make APIs more powerful and flexible without sacrificing consistency.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
Design patterns are proven solutions to common problems. Instead of inventing a new way to filter every time, you use a standard query parameter like ?filter=status:active. Consistent patterns make your API predictable â€” once a developer learns one endpoint, they know how all similar endpoints work.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Standard patterns reduce the learning curve for your API. Developers can switch between endpoints without reading documentation for each one. These patterns also make it easier to build client libraries and SDKs that work across your entire API.

## Common Patterns

| Feature       | Pattern                           | Example                              |
|---------------|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| Filtering     | ?filter=field:value               | ?filter=status:active                |
| Sorting       | ?sort=field,-field                | ?sort=-createdAt,name                |
| Searching     | ?q=search-term                    | ?q=javascript                        |
| Pagination    | ?page=1&perPage=20                | ?page=2&perPage=50                   |
| Partial Resp  | ?fields=id,name,email             | ?fields=id,name                      |
| Embedding     | ?include=related                  | ?include=author,comments             |
| Bulk          | POST /batch with array body       | POST /batch [{...}, {...}]           |`,
