          title: "API Versioning Strategies",
          type: "theory",
          content: `# API Versioning Strategies

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
API versioning allows you to evolve your API without breaking existing clients. Common strategies include URL prefix versioning (/v1/), header versioning (Accept: application/vnd.api+version=2), query parameter versioning (?v=2), and content negotiation. Each has trade-offs for discoverability, caching, and client complexity.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
API versioning is like software versioning for your API. When you make changes that aren't backward compatible (removing a field, changing a response format), you create a new version. Old clients keep using the old version while new clients adopt the new version.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
Without versioning, you can't evolve your API without breaking existing clients. Every breaking change becomes a migration nightmare. Versioning lets you make improvements at your own pace while giving clients time to upgrade.

## Strategies Compared

| Strategy      | Example                    | Pros                    | Cons                    |
|---------------|----------------------------|-------------------------|-------------------------|
| URL Prefix    | /v1/users                  | Easy to discover        | Clutters URLs           |
| Header        | Accept: version=1          | Clean URLs              | Hard to test in browser |
| Query Param   | ?version=1                 | Easy to implement       | No caching granularity  |
| Subdomain     | v1.api.example.com         | Full isolation          | DNS complexity          |
| Content Type  | Accept: app/vnd.api.v1+json | RESTful, cache-friendly | Complex to implement    |`,
