          type: "theory",
          content: `# GraphQL vs REST

ðŸ“š What You'll Learn
GraphQL is a query language for APIs that lets clients request exactly the data they need. Compare GraphQL and REST approaches: single endpoint vs multiple endpoints, over-fetching vs precise queries, and client-driven vs server-driven data retrieval.

ðŸ“– Simple Explanation
REST is like a restaurant with a fixed menu â€” you get what the chef decided to serve. GraphQL is like a buffet where you pick exactly what you want. With REST, the server decides what data comes back. With GraphQL, the client decides.

ðŸŽ¯ Why This Matters
GraphQL solves the over-fetching and under-fetching problems common in REST. Mobile apps especially benefit from GraphQL's precise data retrieval. Facebook, GitHub, Shopify, and many others use GraphQL in production.

## Key Differences

| Aspect | REST | GraphQL |
|--------|------|---------|
| Endpoints | Multiple (/users, /posts) | Single (/graphql) |
| Data Fetching | Server-defined | Client-defined |
| Over-fetching | Common | Eliminated |
| N+1 Problem | Common | Solved with DataLoader |
| Caching | HTTP caching | Custom caching |
| Tooling | Swagger/OpenAPI | GraphiQL/Apollo Studio |`,
