          title: "Modern CSS Selectors & Features",
          type: "practice",
          content: `# Modern CSS Selectors & Features

📚 What You'll Learn
- :is(), :where(), :has() relational selectors
- Container queries for component-level responsiveness
- @layer for controlling cascade order
- Scroll-driven animations (future of CSS)

📖 Simple Explanation
Modern CSS has evolved significantly. :has() is like a "parent selector" - it styles an element based on what it contains. Container queries let components respond to their own container's size, not the viewport. @layer helps organize CSS by giving you control over cascade priority. These features solve long-standing CSS pain points.

🎯 Why This Matters
These modern features reduce JavaScript dependencies, make CSS more powerful and organized, and enable patterns that were previously impossible or required complex workarounds.`,
          order: 3, xpReward: 100, estimatedMinutes: 30, language: "css",
          keyTakeaways: [
            ":has() applies styles to an element if it contains a specific child or follows a selector",
            ":is() and :where() simplify complex selector lists; :where() has zero specificity",
            "Container queries use @container to respond to parent container size, not viewport",
            "@layer lets you control cascade order explicitly"
          ],
          examples: [
            {
              title: "The :has() Selector",
              description: "Parent selector and more with :has()",
              code: "/* Style a card differently if it contains an image */\n.card:has(img) {\n  grid-row: span 2;\n}\n\n/* Style a form group differently if the input is invalid */\n.form-group:has(input:invalid) {\n  border-left: 3px solid red;\n}\n\n/* Style navigation differently if it has dropdowns */\nnav:has(.dropdown) {\n  background: #2c3e50;\n}\n\n/* Style a figure differently if it has a caption */\nfigure:has(figcaption) {\n  background: #f8f9fa;\n  padding: 10px;\n  border-radius: 8px;\n}",
              explanation: ":has() is a relational pseudo-class. The card:has(img) applies styles to .card only if it contains an img element inside it. This enables context-aware styling from parent elements.",
              difficulty: "advanced"
            },
            {
              title: "Container Queries",
              description: "Component-level responsive design",
              code: "/* Define a container */\n.card-container {\n  container-type: inline-size;\n  container-name: card;\n}\n\n/* Query the container's width */\n@container card (min-width: 400px) {\n  .card {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;\n    gap: 20px;\n  }\n}\n\n@container card (min-width: 600px) {\n  .card {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 3fr;\n  }\n  .card img {\n    width: 200px;\n  }\n}\n\n/* Without container queries, the card's layout depended on viewport,\n   not the space available in its container */",
              explanation: "Container queries let components respond to their own container's width. This means a card component looks the same whether it's in a sidebar or a main content area, based on the space it actually has.",
              difficulty: "advanced"
            }
          ],
          commonMistakes: [
            { mistake: "Using :has() without considering performance", explanation: "Complex :has() selectors can be slow on large pages", howToAvoid: "Use :has() judiciously. Simple :has() selectors are fine; avoid deeply nested :has() chains" }
          ],
          codeTemplate: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <style>\n    /* Use :has() and container queries */\n    /* 1. Cards with images should have a different style than cards without */\n    /* 2. Use container queries to change card layout based on available space */\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"card-content\">\n      <h3>No Image Card</h3>\n      <p>This card has no image</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <img src=\"https://via.placeholder.com/150\" alt=\"Placeholder\">\n    <div class=\"card-content\">\n      <h3>With Image</h3>\n      <p>This card has an image</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>",
          solution: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <style>\n    * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }\n    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Arial, sans-serif; padding: 40px; display: flex; gap: 30px; flex-wrap: wrap; }\n    .card { container-type: inline-size; border: 1px solid #e0e0e0; border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; width: 100%; max-width: 500px; }\n    .card:has(img) { border-color: #667eea; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(102,126,234,0.15); }\n    .card-content { padding: 24px; }\n    .card-content h3 { margin-bottom: 8px; color: #1e293b; }\n    .card-content p { color: #64748b; }\n    @container (min-width: 350px) {\n      .card { display: flex; }\n      .card img { width: 150px; object-fit: cover; }\n      .card-content { flex: 1; }\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"card-content\">\n      <h3>No Image Card</h3>\n      <p>This card has no image, so it uses the default style</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <img src=\"https://via.placeholder.com/150\" alt=\"Placeholder\">\n    <div class=\"card-content\">\n      <h3>With Image</h3>\n      <p>This card has :has(img) styling and a container query layout</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>",
