          title: "Color, Contrast & Visual Accessibility",
          type: "practice",
          content: `# Color, Contrast & Visual Accessibility

📚 What You'll Learn
- WCAG color contrast ratios and how to meet them
- Designing for color blindness and low vision
- Accessible color palettes that work for everyone
- Testing tools and techniques

📖 Simple Explanation
Color accessibility means your content is readable by people with various visual abilities. About 1 in 12 men has some form of color blindness (red-green being most common). WCAG guidelines require a contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Never rely solely on color to convey information - always add text labels, icons, or patterns.

🎯 Why This Matters
Good contrast benefits everyone - reading in bright sunlight, on low-quality screens, or with aging eyes. Using only color to convey information excludes color-blind users. Accessible color design is inclusive design.`,
          order: 3, xpReward: 75, estimatedMinutes: 20, language: "css",
